 ## W-buffer bias approximation
+
+### Saturated W depths
+
+F-16's cockpit submits reciprocal W values above the depth buffer's near limit.
+The legacy wrapper clamps an entirely saturated triangle to vertex Z=0.9999.
+With differing RHWs, WineD3D/OpenGL can rasterize those nominally equal depths
+differently, incorrectly rejecting later instrument overlays. A three-triangle
+reproduction loses 6,998 of 6,998 tested overlay pixels on the affected backend.
+Changing vertex Z to 1.0 avoids that rounding but clips on the tested native
+Windows D3D9 driver, so it is not a portable fix.
+
+On shader-model-2-capable devices, saturated triangles retain the existing
+in-range vertex position and use a cached pixel shader that writes exact near
+depth. This preserves texture perspective and depth comparisons. Shader changes
+flush queued geometry first. Devices without shader model 2 retain the legacy
+path. Ordinary depths, W-bias calibration, and partially saturated triangles keep
+their existing handling. The latter can still cross the clip plane.
+
+The regression uses only numeric geometry and a generated white texture; it
+needs no game assets. `detail-probe.exe near-depth` runs the detail regression
+with W buffering enabled to exercise promoted detail shaders.
 
 psVoodoo stores reciprocal W in its D3D depth buffer. An additive constant there
 makes a Glide bias much weaker near the camera and much stronger far away.
